pip-audit 2.10.1, captured 2026-09-19 in a scratch venv (not hand-built), from a real network
install and a real audit run:

    python3 -m venv pa
    pa/bin/pip install -q pip-audit
    printf 'requests==2.19.0\n' > req.txt
    pa/bin/pip-audit -r req.txt -f json --no-deps > pip_audit.json

`--no-deps` was passed, but it does not limit the audit to the one pinned package: pip-audit's
own `--help` describes it as "don't perform any dependency resolution; requires all requirements
are pinned to an exact version" — it changes how pip-audit resolves *unpinned* requirements, not
whether it installs and audits `requests`' own dependencies. The real run still pulled in and
audited `requests==2.19.0`'s four installed dependencies (`chardet`, `idna`, `urllib3`,
`certifi`), five packages total; three of them (`requests`, `idna`, `urllib3`) carry known
vulnerabilities, two (`chardet`, `certifi`) come back with an empty `vulns` list. That is useful
here: the fixture exercises both the vulnerable-dependency path and the clean-dependency path in
one file.

Top-level shape: a **dict** with two keys, `dependencies` (a list) and `fixes` (empty here). Each
`dependencies` entry is `{"name": ..., "version": ..., "vulns": [...]}`; each `vulns` entry is
`{"id": ..., "fix_versions": [...], "aliases": [...], "description": ...}` — matching
`langs/python.py`'s `audit_findings` parser (`dependencies`/`vulns`/`fix_versions`/`aliases`) as
written, no correction needed against this capture.

The capture is stdout only (`> pip_audit.json`); pip-audit's one-line summary ("No known vulnerabilities
found" / "Found N known vulnerabilities in M packages") goes to stderr, which `runner.run` merges into
stdout at run time, so this fixture never showed the line that broke every real run — found live
2026-09-19 and pinned by `test_audit_findings_skips_pip_audits_stderr_summary` in
`test_lang_python.py`.
